    <section class="coty-numbers container-narrow" aria-labelledby="cloud-dancer-numbers">
      <h2 id="cloud-dancer-numbers">Pantone Cloud Dancer in numbers</h2>
      <div class="coty-numbers__spec">
        <div class="coty-numbers__swatch" style={`background-color:${CD}`} aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Pantone</dt><dd>11-4201 Cloud Dancer</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Hex</dt><dd><code>{CD}</code></dd></div>
          <div><dt>RGB</dt><dd><code>{cdRgb.join(", ")}</code></dd></div>
          <div><dt>HSL</dt><dd><code>{cdHsl[0]}°, {cdHsl[1]}%, {cdHsl[2]}%</code></dd></div>
          <div><dt>Relative luminance</dt><dd><code>{luminance(CD).toFixed(3)}</code> (pure white is 1.000)</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </div>
      <p>
        Cloud Dancer is a warm off-white: a little more red than blue, so it reads
        cream beside a cool screen white. The question that decides whether it
        works in a design is what you can put on top of it. These are the WCAG 2
        contrast ratios, computed from the hex codes:
      </p>
      <div class="coty-numbers__table" role="region" aria-labelledby="cloud-dancer-numbers" tabindex="0">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">On Cloud Dancer</th><th scope="col">Contrast</th><th scope="col">What it is good for</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {CD_TEXT.map((t) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row"><span class="coty-numbers__dot" style={`background-color:${t.hex}`} aria-hidden="true"></span>{t.name}<span class="coty-numbers__sub">{t.note}</span></th>
                <td><code>{fmtRatio(t.ratio)}</code></td>
                <td>{verdict(t.ratio)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="coty-numbers__note">
        WCAG asks for 4.5:1 for body text and 3:1 for large text and icons.
        Silhouette is the 2026 pick that works as a text colour on Cloud Dancer;
        last year's Mocha Mousse does not, except at headline size. For print,
        specify PANTONE 11-4201 and let the printer match it: a hex is a screen
        value only. <a href={pairUrl("Cloud Dancer")}>Palettes that pair with Cloud Dancer →</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="coty-trend container-narrow">
      <h2 id="meta-trend">The 2026 meta-trend: calm earth</h2>
      <p>
        Read together, 2026's picks aren't fifteen different directions — they're
        two sides of one mood. On one side is <strong>serene, near-weightless
        light</strong>: Pantone's Cloud Dancer is the first true white it has ever
        crowned, echoed by Dutch Boy's Melodious Ivory and Sherwin-Williams'
        Universal Khaki. On the other is <strong>grounded, tactile earth</strong> —
        warm browns (Benjamin Moore Silhouette, Glidden Warm Mahogany, Minwax
        Special Walnut), deep and soft greens (Dunn-Edwards Midnight Garden, Valspar Warm
        Eucalyptus, PPG's yellow-green Secret Safari) and smokey blue-greens (WGSN/Coloro's Transformative Teal,
        Behr's Hidden Gem, Rust-Oleum's Satin Lagoon).
      </p>
      <p>
        Both camps point the same way: away from the saturated, screen-bright
        palettes of recent years and toward <strong>calm, nature and the
        hand-made</strong> — widely read as an antidote to digital fatigue. The
        teals carry an explicit Earth-first, regenerative message; the whites and
        creams make space; the browns and walnuts bring warmth you can almost
        touch.
      </p>
      <p class="coty-cross">
        Want to build a palette around a 2026 colour? <a href="/colors-that-go-with/">Find what goes with any colour</a>,
        <a href="/browse/">browse the historical archive</a>, or
        <a href="/browse/">explore curated palettes</a> for warm neutrals,
        deep greens and teals.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="coty-yoy container-narrow">
      <h2 id="2025-vs-2026">How 2026 compares with 2025</h2>
      <p class="coty-yoy__answer">
        2025 was brown and plum — Pantone Mocha Mousse, Benjamin Moore Cinnamon
        Slate, Behr Rumors. 2026 swings to calm light and grounded earth: Pantone
        Cloud Dancer is its first-ever white, and the paint brands move from reds
        and plums to khaki, jade, walnut and teal.
      </p>
      <div class="coty-yoy__scroll">
        <table class="coty-yoy__table">
          <caption>Color of the Year by authority, 2025 versus 2026</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Authority</th><th scope="col">2025</th><th scope="col">2026</th><th scope="col">The shift</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {YEAR_OVER_YEAR.map((r) => (
              <tr><th scope="row">{r.brand}</th><td>{r.y2025}</td><td>{r.y2026}</td><td>{r.shift}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="coty-note">
        WGSN + Coloro's Transformative Teal has no like-for-like row because our
        2025 sources don't record their 2025 pick. Sherwin-Williams' 2025 choice
        is its own Color Capsule pick, Grounded; its HGTV Home line named Quietude.
        Outside North America, Dulux named three 2026 colours instead of one — the
        indigo family <em>The Rhythm of Blues</em> (Free Groove, Mellow Flow, Slow
        Swing) — its first palette rather than a single shade, and a second vote
        for the calm end of the spectrum.
      </p>
    </section>

